TEF — EPE‑01‑TEST‑I1

Tacky‑Glue SLEP on Porous Washing‑Machine Foam  
Date: 14‑04
Lineage: EPE‑01 → SLEPE → PF‑SLEP → EPE‑01‑TEST‑I1  
Status: Successful SLEP (foundational PF‑SLEP)
Substrate

The substrate is the interlocking anti‑vibration foam used beneath washing machines. It behaves as an EVA‑adjacent open‑cell composite with a relatively flat working face and a highly porous internal structure. The porosity produces immediate, deep ink absorption once exposed. Mechanical behaviour is compressible and tear‑susceptible when cuts are shallow, but stable enough to maintain a lattice under tension.
Skin Layer

The skin is hand‑applied tacky glue, air‑dried without pressing. It forms a micro‑uneven, glossy, oleophobic surface that naturally resists oil‑based inks. The unevenness becomes the resist mechanism rather than a flaw. Bond strength is high enough to withstand lateral tearing during excision, confirming tacky glue as a viable SLEP skin for porous foams.
Cutting Method

Cuts were made with a vinyl‑blade kiss‑cut in a vertical/horizontal lattice with additional shapes above the grid. Depth was inconsistent, with several cuts too shallow. This caused difficult tear‑outs and some deformation, but the skin remained bonded and the lattice structure held. The foam’s cell structure requires deeper, slower cuts for future PF‑SLEP tests.
Excision Behaviour

Every second cell of the lattice was manually torn out. The exposed foam acted as a hyper‑absorbent ink well, producing a matte, velvety black. The contrast between glossy skin and matte substrate was immediate and strong. Fragile edges required gentle handling but did not collapse, demonstrating a stable two‑layer excision field.
Inking Method

A handmade, somewhat liquid oil‑based engraving ink was applied with soft pressure to avoid damaging fragile edges. The skin rejected ink uniformly due to oleophobicity, while the exposed foam absorbed ink deeply. High contrast was achieved with minimal pressure, confirming the dual‑surface inking behaviour characteristic of SLEPE.
Outcome

This is a full success. The skin formed coherently, the excision revealed a materially distinct substrate, the inking produced strong binary contrast, and the system survived mechanical excision despite rushed cuts. This test establishes the PF‑SLEP branch as a viable and expressive subcategory within EPE‑01 SLEPE.
Lineage Notes

EPE‑01‑TEST‑I1 becomes the foundational entry for porous‑foam SLEPs. The branch is defined by oleophobic skins without pressing, hyper‑absorbent substrates, high contrast with minimal pressure, fragile but expressive excision edges, and suitability for rapid, improvisational SLEP experiments.
